WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.958 --> 00:00:14.809
I danced with Bella for the first time

00:00:14.833 --> 00:00:17.434
as Bei Mir Bistu Shein filled the room.

00:00:17.458 --> 00:00:19.542
And her blue eyes locked with mine.

00:00:19.875 --> 00:00:21.559
We took turns singing

00:00:21.583 --> 00:00:23.018
and forgetting the words.

00:00:23.042 --> 00:00:24.309
She led, I followed.

00:00:24.333 --> 00:00:26.393
A waltz step here,

00:00:26.417 --> 00:00:27.684
a shimmy here.

00:00:27.708 --> 00:00:28.809
(Rattle sound)

00:00:28.833 --> 00:00:30.083
(Laughter)

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:32.434
Hands on our hearts,

00:00:32.458 --> 00:00:33.726
our foreheads touching,

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:36.351
as we communicated
through movement and music,

00:00:36.375 --> 00:00:39.018
making sense through nonsense.

00:00:39.042 --> 00:00:41.934
Bella is 83 and lives with dementia.

00:00:41.958 --> 00:00:45.101
The dance gives us a chance
to find each other.

00:00:45.125 --> 00:00:46.983
How did I learn to dance with dementia?

00:00:47.007 --> 00:00:48.268
Because I'm not a dancer.

00:00:48.292 --> 00:00:49.976
And I'm not a doctor.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.101
But I've played one in the hospital.

00:00:52.125 --> 00:00:53.976
(Laughs)

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.059
I'm a clown doctor.

00:00:56.083 --> 00:00:58.309
Or a medical clown.

00:00:58.333 --> 00:01:01.083
My tools are whoopee cushions,

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:06.518
shakers

00:01:06.542 --> 00:01:08.500
(Shaker rattles)

00:01:09.583 --> 00:01:11.351
and a red nose.

00:01:11.375 --> 00:01:15.476
You know the old adage
"laughter is the best medicine?"

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:16.768
I hear that a lot.

00:01:16.792 --> 00:01:19.393
Now, at the same time,
there are studies to support it,

00:01:19.417 --> 00:01:20.684
but right now,

00:01:20.708 --> 00:01:23.684
I'd like to take you behind the nose
and go beyond the laughter

00:01:23.708 --> 00:01:26.518
and share a few things that I've seen
skating through ICUs.

00:01:26.542 --> 00:01:27.893
In my Heelys.

00:01:27.917 --> 00:01:29.167
Size 11.

00:01:29.917 --> 00:01:32.309
Because I take medical clowning

00:01:32.333 --> 00:01:35.476
very, very seriously.

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:36.643
(Deflating sound)

00:01:36.667 --> 00:01:37.833
(Laughter)

00:01:38.667 --> 00:01:39.817
(Deflating sound)

00:01:40.951 --> 00:01:42.101
(Deflating sound)

00:01:42.125 --> 00:01:43.309
(Laughter)

00:01:43.333 --> 00:01:46.018
My mentor was conducting
clown rounds in the hospital

00:01:46.042 --> 00:01:47.684
when he was approached by a nurse.

00:01:47.708 --> 00:01:49.893
They needed to put a tube
up the kid's nose.

00:01:49.917 --> 00:01:51.184
Kid didn't want to do it,

00:01:51.208 --> 00:01:52.809
so rather than hold the kid down,

00:01:52.833 --> 00:01:54.786
they asked my colleague if he could help.

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:57.101
So the clown asked for a second tube

00:01:57.125 --> 00:01:59.268
and shoved it up his own nose.

00:01:59.292 --> 00:02:00.542
Kid of like this.

00:02:01.583 --> 00:02:03.792
Oh, please don't do this at home.

00:02:10.875 --> 00:02:12.059
(Laughter)

00:02:12.083 --> 00:02:14.809
Now, the kid saw this,

00:02:14.833 --> 00:02:16.601
grabbed his own tube

00:02:16.625 --> 00:02:18.851
and promptly stuck it up his own nose,

00:02:18.875 --> 00:02:20.167
kind of like this.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:31.601
(Applause)

00:02:31.625 --> 00:02:34.101
The clowns, the nurse and the patient

00:02:34.125 --> 00:02:37.643
discovered a creative solution
for their situation together.

00:02:37.667 --> 00:02:40.018
And guess what, there's research
to back this up.

00:02:40.042 --> 00:02:42.809
Randomized controlled trials
in Israel and Italy

00:02:42.833 --> 00:02:45.934
show that medical clowns
can be as effective as tranquilizers

00:02:45.958 --> 00:02:47.351
with no side effects.

00:02:47.375 --> 00:02:50.393
In 2004, I started conducting
my own clown rounds

00:02:50.417 --> 00:02:53.601
at the Memorial Sloan Kettering
Cancer Center in New York City.

00:02:53.625 --> 00:02:56.684
My colleague and I were invited
to accompany a young six-year-old

00:02:56.708 --> 00:02:58.726
with the most adorable southern accent,

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:01.643
to accompany him while he got
his chemotherapy port flushed,

00:03:01.667 --> 00:03:03.726
a very uncomfortable
and regular procedure.

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:06.184
We joined him, his mom and the nurse

00:03:06.208 --> 00:03:09.101
in this tiny, closed curtain cubicle.

00:03:09.125 --> 00:03:12.934
Every medical clown encounter begins
by obtaining consent from the patient.

00:03:12.958 --> 00:03:15.309
So we ask him if we can be there.

00:03:15.333 --> 00:03:16.601
He says, "Sure."

00:03:16.625 --> 00:03:19.184
We're often the one element
that a child could control

00:03:19.208 --> 00:03:20.684
while they're in the hospital.

00:03:20.708 --> 00:03:22.851
So we start with a card trick,

00:03:22.875 --> 00:03:24.351
fan the deck so he can pick.

00:03:24.375 --> 00:03:26.809
But as soon as the nurse
approaches with the needle

00:03:26.833 --> 00:03:28.101
to flush the port,

00:03:28.125 --> 00:03:29.768
he starts screaming and cussing

00:03:29.792 --> 00:03:32.351
like no six-year-old
I'd ever heard in my life.

00:03:32.375 --> 00:03:34.476
So we say, "Hey, should we
come back later?"

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:36.934
He stops, mouth open,

00:03:36.958 --> 00:03:40.518
eyes wet with tears,
face flushed pink with anger,

00:03:40.542 --> 00:03:41.851
and he smiles,

00:03:41.875 --> 00:03:44.851
"Oh no, you're fine,
I want you to be here."

00:03:44.875 --> 00:03:46.143
OK.

00:03:46.167 --> 00:03:47.518
So we start playing a song,

00:03:47.542 --> 00:03:50.518
my colleague on recorder,
me on whoopee cushion.

00:03:50.542 --> 00:03:52.351
The nurse approaches with the needle,

00:03:52.375 --> 00:03:55.125
and it happens again,
this torrent of four-letter words.

00:03:56.583 --> 00:03:58.601
He went from playing and laughing

00:03:58.625 --> 00:04:00.059
to screaming and crying,

00:04:00.083 --> 00:04:02.393
back and forth until
the procedure was complete.

00:04:02.417 --> 00:04:03.684
For the first time,

00:04:03.708 --> 00:04:07.476
I experienced this odd duality
of joy and suffering.

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:08.893
But not for the last time.

00:04:08.917 --> 00:04:11.893
See, when we're there,
we're not there merely to distract

00:04:11.917 --> 00:04:13.809
or make anyone feel better per se.

00:04:13.833 --> 00:04:16.101
The medical clowns work moment by moment

00:04:16.125 --> 00:04:18.684
to create connections between the clowns,

00:04:18.708 --> 00:04:21.226
the nurse, the parent and the child.

00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:24.559
This provides a source of power
or control for the child

00:04:24.583 --> 00:04:27.268
while supporting the staff
with their work.

00:04:27.292 --> 00:04:30.143
I've spent over a decade
bringing joy and delight

00:04:30.167 --> 00:04:32.226
to the bedsides of terminally ill children

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:34.060
in the top hospitals in New York City.

00:04:34.084 --> 00:04:35.668
And you know what I've learned?

00:04:36.625 --> 00:04:38.393
Everyone's hurting.

00:04:38.417 --> 00:04:41.351
Staff, family, patients.

00:04:41.375 --> 00:04:43.934
The patient's in the hospital
because they're hurting.

00:04:43.958 --> 00:04:46.643
The family's hurting
as they navigate uncertainty, grief

00:04:46.667 --> 00:04:48.351
and the financial burdens of care.

00:04:48.375 --> 00:04:50.726
The staff is hurting,
only it's more than burnout.

00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:52.351
More and more health care workers

00:04:52.375 --> 00:04:55.851
are reporting feeling overworked
and overextended.

00:04:55.875 --> 00:04:58.059
Now, I'm not so naive as to suggest

00:04:58.083 --> 00:05:00.833
that the solution is
to send in the clowns.

00:05:02.750 --> 00:05:04.750
(Laughter)

00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:07.018
But what if?

00:05:07.042 --> 00:05:11.101
What if the tools of medical clown
arts practitioners from around the world

00:05:11.125 --> 00:05:13.559
permeated our entire health care system?

00:05:13.583 --> 00:05:17.518
In 2018, at the Healthcare Clowning
International Meeting,

00:05:17.542 --> 00:05:21.809
they represented over 150 programs
in 50 different countries.

00:05:21.833 --> 00:05:23.393
University of Haifa

00:05:23.417 --> 00:05:27.851
offers a formal undergraduate
degree program in medical clowning.

00:05:27.875 --> 00:05:32.476
Argentina has passed laws
requiring the presence of medical clowns

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:34.976
in public hospitals
at their largest province.

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:37.101
And this work affects
more than the patients.

00:05:37.125 --> 00:05:39.684
It makes things better
for the whole health care team.

00:05:39.708 --> 00:05:42.018
One of my favorite games
to play in the hospital

00:05:42.042 --> 00:05:43.309
is elevator music.

00:05:43.333 --> 00:05:45.851
I love elevators, because they're a place

00:05:45.875 --> 00:05:48.934
where paths cross, different worlds meet.

00:05:48.958 --> 00:05:50.601
It's intimate,

00:05:50.625 --> 00:05:52.143
uncomfortably quiet

00:05:52.167 --> 00:05:55.143
and just begging
for a little playful disruption.

00:05:55.167 --> 00:05:56.518
The doors close

00:05:56.542 --> 00:06:00.101
and "The Girl from Ipanema"
starts playing on Hammond organ,

00:06:00.125 --> 00:06:04.018
because I keep a portable speaker
hidden in my pocket.

00:06:04.042 --> 00:06:07.184
So for those used to using
the silent, sterile elevator,

00:06:07.208 --> 00:06:08.518
it's a moment of surprise.

00:06:08.542 --> 00:06:11.917
Folks have permission
to acknowledge or not this disruption.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:15.851
The game grows with every stop,

00:06:15.875 --> 00:06:18.268
because as soon as the elevator stops,

00:06:18.292 --> 00:06:19.559
the music stops.

00:06:19.583 --> 00:06:20.851
New passengers get on,

00:06:20.875 --> 00:06:24.434
and the current passengers
get to witness the new passengers --

00:06:24.458 --> 00:06:27.893
their surprise -- as they hear
the elevator music for the first time.

00:06:27.917 --> 00:06:31.768
You experience the shift of adults

00:06:31.792 --> 00:06:35.393
standing silently,
strangers in an elevator,

00:06:35.417 --> 00:06:37.684
to attempting to suppress their mirth,

00:06:37.708 --> 00:06:40.184
to, "Is this a party or an elevator,"

00:06:40.208 --> 00:06:42.684
filled with full-on laughter.

00:06:42.708 --> 00:06:45.184
Research conducted in Brazil,

00:06:45.208 --> 00:06:47.684
Australia, Canada and Germany

00:06:47.708 --> 00:06:50.809
confirm that the artistic
interventions of medical clowns

00:06:50.833 --> 00:06:53.893
improve the work environment
for the staff, beyond the elevator,

00:06:53.917 --> 00:06:56.059
and support their work administering care.

00:06:56.083 --> 00:06:57.893
Promising research in the US indicates

00:06:57.917 --> 00:07:00.018
that arts programing in the hospital

00:07:00.042 --> 00:07:01.684
can improve the work environment,

00:07:01.708 --> 00:07:03.601
leading to increased job satisfaction

00:07:03.625 --> 00:07:05.351
and better quality of care.

00:07:05.375 --> 00:07:06.768
My work has taught me

00:07:06.792 --> 00:07:09.559
how to actually be present.

00:07:09.583 --> 00:07:12.393
How to breathe in a room
with a person in pain.

00:07:12.417 --> 00:07:13.684
How to connect

00:07:13.708 --> 00:07:17.268
and build trust, no matter
the age, ability or illness.

00:07:17.292 --> 00:07:20.197
And how medical clowning
is an excellent way of using the arts

00:07:20.221 --> 00:07:22.768
to put the care back in health care.

00:07:22.792 --> 00:07:24.059
Thanks.

00:07:24.083 --> 00:07:27.708
(Applause)

